---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allPeople, interactiveCount } from '../lib/people';
const people=await allPeople();const interactive=await interactiveCount();
---
<Base title="About the Pantheon" description="An expanding, sourced atlas of scientific discoveries and philosophical ideas, designed for active learning."><section class="shell page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">FOR THE ENDLESSLY CURIOUS</p><h1>Ideas worth<br/><em>understanding.</em></h1><p>The Pantheon is a personal learning project: a growing reference to people who changed how we understand the world, with experiments and arguments you can operate.</p></section><article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 longform"><h2>A growing archive</h2><p>The current collection contains {people.length} profiles and {interactive} interactive contributions. The long-term ambition is an extensive global archive. This release is a curated foundation, with substantial gaps still to fill across regions, periods, genders, and fields.</p><h2>How to use it</h2><p>Browse by field, era, or region. Use the timeline to place contributions in context, or follow a learning path. In the laboratory, predict an outcome before moving a control. On a philosophy profile, reject a premise and follow the consequences.</p><h2>Sources before slogans</h2><p>Every profile includes sources. Short biographies introduce a subject; the linked texts and scholarly accounts support deeper reading. Approximate dates remain approximate. We distinguish historical achievements from later interpretations and identify disputed priority claims.</p><h2>About the portraits</h2><p>Portraits are historical photographs, paintings, sculptures, or later artistic depictions sourced from Wikimedia. Ancient thinkers generally have no authenticated contemporary likeness. A portrait is not evidence of exactly what its subject looked like; follow the credit on the profile for its provenance.</p><h2>Models have boundaries</h2><p>Simulations simplify the world. The laboratory explains each model’s assumptions, including illustrative scales and idealised conditions. An argument map reconstructs a line of reasoning; a supported conclusion in the map is not a declaration that it is true.</p><h2>Your learning stays with you</h2><p>Saved profiles, read status, and notes are stored locally in this browser. There is no account or cross-device sync. Clearing browser storage removes this data. If storage is unavailable, notebook controls report that changes cannot be persisted.</p><h2>Keep asking</h2><p>Discoveries depend on collaborators, instruments, institutions, and earlier traditions. These profiles are starting points for inquiry, not a claim that history is made by isolated geniuses.</p><p><a href="/explore">Find your next question →</a></p></article></Base>
